# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) to switch the optional signup on for a build.
# Without a .env, the field still works and nothing is sent anywhere — which is
# what every fork, contributor and local dev build gets.

# Anything that accepts a POST of {"email": "...", "name": "..."}.
VITE_SIGNUP_ENDPOINT=

# Sent as the `x-shortlisted-key` header. Any long random string.
VITE_SIGNUP_TOKEN=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE TREATING EITHER VALUE AS SECRET
#
# They are not secret, and cannot be. A Chrome extension is a zip of JavaScript:
# anyone can install it from the Web Store, unpack it, and read every string in
# it. Vite INLINES these at build time, so they end up as literals in the
# bundle. There is no way to ship a secret in a client — not in .env, not
# obfuscated, not anywhere.
#
# What .env genuinely buys you: the values stay out of the public repository.
# Forks don't post to your list, and a casual reader of the source doesn't get a
# URL to play with. That is worth having. It is not a security control.
#
# So the endpoint must be able to defend itself. Whatever you point it at should:
#
#   1. APPEND ONLY. It must never be able to read the list back. If the URL
#      leaks, the worst case is junk rows — not someone downloading your users.
#   2. RATE LIMIT per IP. A dozen a day is generous for a real signup.
#   3. VALIDATE server-side. Email shape, length caps, reject anything unknown.
#   4. CHECK THE TOKEN, and drop mismatches silently. Stops drive-by traffic;
#      will not stop anyone who unpacked the extension. Defence in depth only.
#   5. DEDUPE on email, so a double-click is one row.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GOOGLE SHEETS, THE TWO WAYS
#
# A) Apps Script — free, ~15 minutes, no infrastructure.
#    Extensions > Apps Script on the sheet, doPost(e) that checks the token and
#    appends a row, deploy as a Web App with access "Anyone". The URL goes in
#    VITE_SIGNUP_ENDPOINT. Simple, and the sheet's credentials are never
#    exposed — the script runs as you. Weakness: no rate limiting, so if the URL
#    is found you can collect junk rows until you rotate it.
#
# B) Cloudflare Worker in front of the sheet — free tier, ~30 lines.
#    The Worker holds the Google credentials as a REAL server-side secret,
#    rate limits, validates, then appends. This is the version that actually
#    holds up, and it's what to move to if (A) starts collecting noise.
#
# Either way rotating is cheap: change the URL/token, ship an update.
#
# Or skip both and point this at Tally / Formspree / Buttondown, which already
# do 1-5 and handle deletion requests for you (which matters under GDPR).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
